WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:02.787
Hello, today we introduce DTCA,

2
00:00:02.787 --> 00:00:06.930
which assigns learning credit based on how much tokens help refine an answer

3
00:00:06.930 --> 00:00:08.609
in diffusion language models.

4
00:00:09.692 --> 00:00:13.279
To see the intuition, consider the question, “What happened at lunch?”

5
00:00:13.779 --> 00:00:15.950
We might first think of “ate” and “apples,”

6
00:00:15.950 --> 00:00:19.015
then add details: “Mia ate three red apples.”

7
00:00:19.515 --> 00:00:22.429
Here, the verb and noun carry the core information.

8
00:00:22.929 --> 00:00:26.180
This suggests that different parts of speech, or POS,

9
00:00:26.180 --> 00:00:28.432
may contribute differently to an answer.

10
00:00:28.932 --> 00:00:31.162
Now consider diffusion language models,

11
00:00:31.162 --> 00:00:35.748
which build answers from an entirely masked sequence through iterative denoising.

12
00:00:36.248 --> 00:00:39.359
Do different parts of speech contribute differently here too?

13
00:00:40.442 --> 00:00:46.281
To investigate this, we analyze DLM-generated answers on GSM8K.

14
00:00:46.701 --> 00:00:50.289
Figure a shows different commitment patterns across parts of speech.

15
00:00:50.709 --> 00:00:54.180
Figure b shows how much they improve confidence at the remaining masks

16
00:00:54.180 --> 00:00:55.411
after token commitment.

17
00:00:55.831 --> 00:00:58.734
Verbs are prominent early and yield the largest gains,

18
00:00:58.734 --> 00:01:01.172
followed by nouns and proper nouns.

19
00:01:01.592 --> 00:01:05.075
These findings show that different parts of speech contribute differently.

20
00:01:05.750 --> 00:01:08.722
Based on these findings, we propose DTCA.

21
00:01:09.142 --> 00:01:11.313
We start by estimating contribution.

22
00:01:12.400 --> 00:01:15.465
Committing the noun “bag” raises summed prediction confidence

23
00:01:15.465 --> 00:01:19.656
at the same remaining masks from 0.9 to 1.5.

24
00:01:20.256 --> 00:01:23.495
This gain of 0.6 measures its contribution.

25
00:01:24.095 --> 00:01:29.192
We then average contributions within each POS category and convert them into weights.

26
00:01:30.275 --> 00:01:34.060
With these weights in hand, we can choose which states to train on.

27
00:01:35.150 --> 00:01:38.273
We rank states by contribution-weighted uncertainty

28
00:01:38.273 --> 00:01:41.164
and select the most valuable time steps for training.

29
00:01:42.233 --> 00:01:45.972
Next, we assign token credit within the selected state.

30
00:01:47.025 --> 00:01:51.959
Within this state, we weight committed tokens and retained masked targets by POS,

31
00:01:51.959 --> 00:01:53.933
then normalize these weights.

32
00:01:54.533 --> 00:01:59.316
Multiplying the shared advantage by each weight gives us token-level learning signals.

33
00:02:00.400 --> 00:02:01.967
Before using these signals,

34
00:02:01.967 --> 00:02:05.369
we adjust the token credits to balance their effects on entropy.

35
00:02:06.442 --> 00:02:09.948
In this example, entropy increases total 0.2,

36
00:02:09.948 --> 00:02:12.607
while decreases total 0.6.

37
00:02:13.207 --> 00:02:16.864
We rescale the credits so both totals become 0.3.

38
00:02:17.942 --> 00:02:22.470
DTCA outperforms the base model across all tested generation budgets.

39
00:02:22.890 --> 00:02:28.718
With 128 denoising steps, accuracy improves by 9.0 percentage points on MATH500

40
00:02:28.718 --> 00:02:31.167
and 5.3 on GSM8K.

41
00:02:32.233 --> 00:02:34.683
To conclude, we make three contributions.

42
00:02:35.383 --> 00:02:40.828
First, we analyze POS differences in DLM denoising and refinement.

43
00:02:41.528 --> 00:02:46.021
Second, DTCA uses these signals for state selection and token credit,

44
00:02:46.021 --> 00:02:49.539
with entropy calibration adapted to DLMs.

45
00:02:50.239 --> 00:02:54.674
Third, our experiments validate DTCA and its key components.

46
00:02:56.108 --> 00:03:00.369
Thank you for watching, and we look forward to your feedback on DTCA.
